## The blocker: 5 committed tests contradict the owner decision

The tests read the shipped week files and assert that weeks 2 to 8 stay draft. Daniel approved them
on 2026-11-01, so the shipped data now holds 8 approved weeks. No implementation makes both true.

1. `dashboard/__tests__/storytime-print-covers-16books-adversarial.test.ts:141`
   test "ADV-D12: approved-only values keep the week 1 story but lose every draft-week title, and the
   rule sees mixed source case"
   `expect(approved.length, 'Draft weeks ship beside the approved week.').toBeLessThan(weeks.length)`
   Received: expected 8 to be less than 8.
2. `dashboard/__tests__/storytime-print-covers-16books-adversarial.test.ts:283`
   test "ADV-D12: the mock values strictly extend the approved values, so a passing print would render
   draft-week words"
   `expect(missing.length, 'The print context still lacks a draft-only field today, so the D12 stop
   fires first.').toBeGreaterThan(0)`
   Received: expected 0 to be greater than 0.
3. `dashboard/__tests__/storytime-print-covers-16books.test.ts:793`
   test "R22: mock reports draft sources; print rejects draft-only field values and preserves v1 and
   v2 outputs"
   `expect(report, 'The build report identifies draft source weeks.').toMatch(/draft/i)`
   Received: the report holds no "draft" text, because every L2 U1 week is approved.
4. `dashboard/__tests__/storytime-print-covers-16books.test.ts:985`
   test "R22 M21 and M22: print source and version output path remain bounded"
   `expect(result.status, 'A print build cannot use draft-week values (M21).').not.toBe(0)`
   Received: the print build exits 0, because the D12 stop has no draft-only field to stop.
5. `dashboard/__tests__/storytime-print-contract-v2.test.ts:273`
   test "keeps the other parts draft in all eight weeks"
   `expect(approval[part], 'Week ${week}: ${part} stays draft.').toEqual({ status: 'draft' })`
   Received: `{ status: 'approved', date: '2026-11-01', words: '3. Covers: Approved', source: … }`
   for `text` of weeks 2 to 8.

Green did not edit a test. The word "draft" in the test names and the test text is not a defect in
the code. The tests hardcode the approval state of the shipped data. Rule 11.3 makes new tests load
the shipped data. These tests must build their own draft week in a temporary copy instead, as R22
already does with `--data`.

